Sonos is working on an upgraded version of its Ace headphones that will support AI, reports <em><a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-08-11/sonos-plans-ace-ultra-headphones-and-broad-push-into-ai">Bloomberg</a></em>. The Sonos Ace Ultra showed up in an FCC filing earlier this week, with the product likely set to debut in September.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2024/09/sonos-ace-purple.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-964245" /><br/>
Sonos debuted the original <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2024/05/21/hands-on-sonos-ace-headphones/">Sonos Ace</a> in 2024. Reviews praised the sound quality and active noise cancellation, but the headphone market is crowded, and Sonos' product launched shortly after its <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2024/07/25/sonos-app-update-apology/">disastrous app redesign</a>. The headphones also didn't fully integrate with the rest of the Sonos ecosystem, making them unappealing to Sonos users. Sonos ended up selling around 200,000 units, well below the internal one-million target it set.<br/>
<br/>
<em>Bloomberg</em> suggests the new model will kick off Sonos' push into artificial intelligence. The headphones could support voice commands based on info in the filing, allowing them to be used with AI chatbots from third-party companies. Sonos CEO Tom Conrad said last month that AI will be an area of focus, with "conversational computing and predictive intelligence moving into the home."<br/>
<br/>
The headphones will come in Greige, Shadow Black, Agave, Blush, and Sand, according to <a href="https://apps.fcc.gov/eas/GetApplicationAttachment.html?id=9521921">the FCC filing</a>, but little else is known about them because most of the filing was redacted.<br/>
<br/>
